(ns examples.i64-shift)

;; A shift count is a guest literal, and the two compiler hosts represent a
;; guest literal differently: a `long` on the JVM, a JavaScript `bigint` under
;; nbb. `kotoba-verifier` re-derives the "count must be an integer LITERAL in
;; [0,63]" rule -- the restriction that lets the native backends lower these
;; onto CL / x1 with no range check -- and until kotoba-verifier 3d7a6f0 it
;; re-derived it with bare `integer?`, which is false for a bigint. So on the
;; JDK-free route the count of EVERY shift failed the literal test and NO
;; artifact using one could be built, while `clojure -M:run` compiled the same
;; source. This file is the fixture that makes that route regression visible.
;;
;; All three i64 shifts, because they are three separate table entries. The
;; argument is signed and the counts differ so that the three cannot be
;; confused for each other: at x = -8 the result is
;;   (-8 << 3) + ((-8 >> 1) + (u64(-8) >>> 63)) = -64 + -4 + 1 = -67
;; and the `+1` is contributed only by the LOGICAL right shift -- swap
;; `u64-shift-right` for `i64-shift-right` and it becomes -68. Measured by
;; executing the artifact under the W^X loader, not computed and trusted.
;;
;; i32 shifts are deliberately absent: their gate had the identical defect and
;; is fixed with the same predicate, but `:i32` typed values are not admitted
;; on native at all ("typed values currently require the kotoba-script web
;; target, typed Wasm target, or qualified native ... features"), so there is
;; no native artifact to execute. That half is pinned in kotoba-verifier's own
;; `verifier_shift_literal_test.cljc`, which runs on both hosts.

(defn mixed [x]
  (+ (i64-shift-left x 3)
     (+ (i64-shift-right x 1)
        (u64-shift-right x 63))))

(defn main [] (mixed -8))
